<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.11.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01451</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.11.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e9eca65b-eb79-494a-bf54-5bbdf689e788</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="21122930-8849-43cd-aa50-27f26db8ae48" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
  <!--text input-->
  <exercise data-content-id="8e0f0500-55dd-4440-9978-28a0af0a9367" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="61">
    <problem>
      <para>For questions 1 – 4, use the following:</para>
      <para>Function \(h\) describes the height of a ball, in inches, after \(n\)<emphasis> </emphasis>bounces and is defined
        by the equation \(h(n)=120 \cdot (\frac{4}{5})^n\).</para>
      <para> What is \(h(3)\)? Round to the nearest integer. Think about what it represents in this situation.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.2 – 5.11.3. Are you using it
        the right way? </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="6d7401c6-c597-45e1-815d-25b0c4ade80f" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="No" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Could \(h(n)\) be 150? </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.2 – 5.11.3 to test your understanding? Then come
        back and try this question again.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="10c85cd5-5b32-428f-922e-29567dcc1617" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="the ball modeled by f(n)" data-solution-options="[&quot;the ball modeled by f(n)&quot;, &quot;the ball modeled by h(n)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which ball loses its height more quickly, this ball, modeled by \(h(n)=120 \cdot (\frac{4}{5})^n\), or a
          tennis ball whose height in inches after \(n\) bounces is modeled by the function \(f\) where \(f(n)=50 \cdot
          (\frac{5}{9})^n\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.2 – 5.11.3. Are you using it
        the right way? </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="e7b5aa97-329f-4653-b67e-535dcc64da64" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="11">
    <problem>
      <para>How many bounces would it take before the ball, modeled by \(h(n)\), bounces less than 12 inches from the
          surface?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.2 – 5.11.3 to test your understanding? Then come
        back and try this question again. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="2f20d8bb-d3db-4e5e-b972-61d20ef47f9a" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="109" data-solution-options="[&quot;560&quot;, &quot;56&quot;, &quot;21&quot;, &quot;109&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>After a second bounce, a ball reached a height of 70 cm. The rebound factor for the ball was 0.8. From
          approximately what height, in cm, was the ball dropped?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 109 cm</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.4 – 5.11.5. Are you using it
        the right way?</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q6-->
  <exercise data-content-id="133a4d47-85ea-4592-98ce-f24d7e3a3aa1" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="150">
    <problem>
      <para>Using the table below, find the height, in cm, at which the ball was dropped.


        </para>
      <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
        <thead/>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">
              Number of Bounces</entry>
            <entry>
              1
            </entry>
            <entry>
              2
            </entry>
            <entry>
              3
            </entry>
            <entry>
              4
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">Height in cm</entry>
            <entry>
              90
            </entry>
            <entry>
              54
            </entry>
            <entry>
              32
            </entry>
            <entry>
              19
            </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.4 – 5.11.5 to test your understanding? Then come
        back and try this question again.
      </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution> </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response"> </div>
</div>
  </section>
